You are assisting with business process modelling. A first check could not decide whether the conditions in the list below hold for the business process described below. They are conditions of the rule [ECD-Art10-1]. Look again.

INSTRUCTIONS
- The legal passages below are all the rules found for this process. Use them only to understand what a condition means (for example a definition of a word). Whether a condition holds must still be shown by the process description.
- For each condition, choose its status:
  - "confirmed": the description states that it holds. A statement in legal terms is "confirmed" when the description states the same fact in everyday words.
  - "contradicted": the description states something that makes it false.
  - "not_stated": use this only when no sentence of the description speaks to the statement.
- "confirmed" needs a quote that itself states that fact; a sentence about a different event never confirms it.
- Do not guess facts the description does not state.
- quotes: for "confirmed" or "contradicted", a list of 1 to 3 sentences of the process description that together show it, each copied exactly and word for word. Use more than one sentence only when the fact is stated across several sentences. For "not_stated", use an empty list []. Quotes are checked automatically; a quote that does not appear in the description is ignored, and an item with no such quote counts as "not_stated".
- Give one result for every condition, with its id, and no other results.
- Answer with one JSON object that follows the JSON schema below. Output only the JSON, with no other text.

EXAMPLE OF EVERYDAY WORDS (made up, from another domain)
Statement: "C1: the vessel has obtained clearance for departure from the port authority"
Description sentence: "The harbour master tells the captain that the ship may leave."
Result: "confirmed", quotes ["The harbour master tells the captain that the ship may leave"] (the same fact in everyday words). The sentence "The crew loads the cargo." speaks about a different event and would not confirm it.

JSON SCHEMA
{
  "$defs": {
    "ItemCheck": {
      "properties": {
        "id": {
          "description": "The id of the item, as given in the list",
          "title": "Id",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "closest_sentence": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "The sentence of the process description that comes closest to the statement, copied word for word, or null if none",
          "title": "Closest Sentence"
        },
        "status": {
          "description": "confirmed, contradicted or not_stated, judged from the description only",
          "enum": [
            "confirmed",
            "not_stated",
            "contradicted"
          ],
          "title": "Status",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "quotes": {
          "description": "1 to 3 sentences copied word for word from the process description that together show the status; empty list for not_stated",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "maxItems": 3,
          "title": "Quotes",
          "type": "array"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "id",
        "closest_sentence",
        "status",
        "quotes"
      ],
      "title": "ItemCheck",
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "results": {
      "description": "One result for every item of the list",
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/ItemCheck"
      },
      "title": "Results",
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "results"
  ],
  "title": "CheckOutputV2",
  "type": "object"
}

PROCESS DESCRIPTION
Title: Ordering in an Online Shop  

A customer that logs into an E-Shop system and has to wait for its login confirmation (its credentials are checked by the system).
If the login was successful then the customer can continue to select products, else the shopping experience stops.
After selecting a product the customer has to add it to a shopping cart, save the product and check if every product was already selected.
These steps repeat until all products were selected.
Then the order is finished by the shopping system and simultaneously payment and shipment for the order is prepared.
For the payment the customer has to enter its payment data and has to wait until the bank confirms the payment.
While waiting for the payment confirmation the customer can enter its shipping address (an independent billing address can be entered if the shipping address is not equal to the billing address).
Finally if the address and the payment steps are executed then the order will be finished by the system.
The E-Shop belongs to a company in Austria and sells to private persons; its website is available in German and English.

LEGAL PASSAGES
[ECD-Art10-2] e-Commerce Directive (2000/31/EC) - Article 10: Information to be provided
2. Member States shall ensure that, except when otherwise agreed by parties who are not consumers, the service provider indicates any relevant codes of conduct to which he subscribes and information on how those codes can be consulted electronically.

[ECD-Art10-3] e-Commerce Directive (2000/31/EC) - Article 10: Information to be provided
3. Contract terms and general conditions provided to the recipient must be made available in a way that allows him to store and reproduce them.

[ECD-Art10-4] e-Commerce Directive (2000/31/EC) - Article 10: Information to be provided
4. Paragraphs 1 and 2 shall not apply to contracts concluded exclusively by exchange of electronic mail or by equivalent individual communications.

CONDITIONS
C1: the recipient of the service is about to place an order
